1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,727
Can we predict whether a molecule is

2
00:00:02,727 --> 00:00:05,454
worth testing before we even make it?

3
00:00:05,454 --> 00:00:08,571
That question matters because a promising idea
on

4
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:11,688
a computer still has to become something real.

5
00:00:11,688 --> 00:00:14,805
Someone must work out how to make the

6
00:00:14,805 --> 00:00:18,312
molecule, produce it and test what it actually
does.

7
00:00:18,312 --> 00:00:21,818
We cannot give every possible design the same
attention.

8
00:00:21,818 --> 00:00:24,935
AI offers an interesting possibility: use
patterns learned

9
00:00:24,935 --> 00:00:28,052
from existing data to suggest designs worth
investigating.

10
00:00:28,052 --> 00:00:32,338
But a suggested structure is not yet an
experimentally confirmed hit.

11
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:36,234
We will follow one liver cancer study from
design to

12
00:00:36,234 --> 00:00:40,520
testing and improvement, then ask what AI
contributed, which benefits remain

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:44,416
possible rather than proven, and what better
evidence would require.

14
00:00:44,416 --> 00:00:49,481
Our question is not whether a computer can
finish drug development by itself.

15
00:00:49,481 --> 00:00:54,546
It is whether algorithms can help researchers
reach a more useful starting point.

16
00:00:54,546 --> 00:00:58,442
Before we ask how a molecule is designed,
though, we

17
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:01,948
need to know what we want it to do.

18
00:01:01,948 --> 00:01:05,844
The disease in this case is hepatocellular
carcinoma, or HCC,

19
00:01:05,844 --> 00:01:09,740
the most common type of primary liver cancer in
adults.

20
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:13,247
Primary means that the cancer starts in the
liver.

21
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:16,753
We are not covering every cancer or every
treatment.

22
00:01:16,753 --> 00:01:19,870
Our focus is one early research task: finding

23
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:22,987
molecules that might affect a protein called
CDK20.

24
00:01:22,987 --> 00:01:26,883
A protein is one of the working components
inside cells.

25
00:01:26,883 --> 00:01:30,000
When researchers choose a protein they hope a

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,507
molecule will act on, they call it a target.

27
00:01:33,507 --> 00:01:37,013
The target and the candidate molecule are
different things.

28
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:40,909
CDK20 is the object researchers want to affect;
a candidate

29
00:01:40,909 --> 00:01:44,416
is a possible chemical design they want to test.

30
00:01:44,416 --> 00:01:49,091
CDK20 did not become interesting simply because
an AI selected its name.

31
00:01:49,091 --> 00:01:52,987
Earlier research had linked it to HCC and
investigated what

32
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:57,273
happened when its activity or expression was
changed in experimental models.

33
00:01:57,273 --> 00:02:01,558
In the 2023 study, disease-related data analysis
helped prioritise targets, and

34
00:02:01,558 --> 00:02:05,844
researchers combined that information with
existing biological evidence to choose CDK20.

35
00:02:05,844 --> 00:02:09,740
That choice does not make CDK20 the single cause
of

36
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:13,636
liver cancer, or prove that blocking it will
treat patients.

37
00:02:13,636 --> 00:02:17,533
It gives the team a focused question: can we
find

38
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:21,818
a molecule that interacts with this target and
deserves further investigation?

39
00:02:21,818 --> 00:02:24,935
A target name makes the task more specific.

40
00:02:24,935 --> 00:02:27,663
But a name alone does not tell

41
00:02:27,663 --> 00:02:30,390
us what shape a molecule should have.

42
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:32,338
Molecules interact in three-dimensional space.

43
00:02:32,338 --> 00:02:35,844
A useful design must do more than look
interesting

44
00:02:35,844 --> 00:02:39,351
on paper: it needs suitable interactions with
the target.

45
00:02:39,351 --> 00:02:42,468
Knowing the protein shape can help researchers
think

46
00:02:42,468 --> 00:02:45,974
about where and how a small molecule might bind.

47
00:02:45,974 --> 00:02:50,649
This is where we must separate two different
structures: the shape of

48
00:02:50,649 --> 00:02:54,935
the target protein, and the structure of the
molecule being designed.

49
00:02:54,935 --> 00:02:59,221
The team used a predicted CDK20 structure from
the AlphaFold database.

50
00:02:59,221 --> 00:03:03,506
In broad terms, AlphaFold uses protein
sequences, information from related sequences

51
00:03:03,506 --> 00:03:07,792
and patterns learned from known structures to
predict a three-dimensional model.

52
00:03:07,792 --> 00:03:10,909
That is different from measuring the structure
experimentally.

53
00:03:10,909 --> 00:03:15,584
The output is a prediction, not a direct
photograph of the protein.

54
00:03:15,584 --> 00:03:18,311
A sequence is the order of the

55
00:03:18,311 --> 00:03:21,039
building blocks that make up a protein.

56
00:03:21,039 --> 00:03:26,104
Knowing that order and knowing the folded shape
are different kinds of information.

57
00:03:26,104 --> 00:03:28,052
Structure prediction helps connect them.

58
00:03:28,052 --> 00:03:30,779
The researchers did not ask the model

59
00:03:30,779 --> 00:03:33,507
to discover the disease or invent CDK20.

60
00:03:33,507 --> 00:03:38,182
They used structural information to help ask a
more focused chemical question.

61
00:03:38,182 --> 00:03:41,299
The researchers also examined uncertainty in
that model.

62
00:03:41,299 --> 00:03:46,364
They removed a low-confidence region at one end
before continuing the design work.

63
00:03:46,364 --> 00:03:49,481
We do not need to memorise its position.

64
00:03:49,481 --> 00:03:52,208
The important point is that a model

65
00:03:52,208 --> 00:03:54,935
did not remove the need for judgement.

66
00:03:54,935 --> 00:03:57,663
Researchers still had to decide which
information

67
00:03:57,663 --> 00:04:00,390
was useful enough for the next step.

68
00:04:00,390 --> 00:04:04,286
The predicted structure helped the team start
structure-guided design without

69
00:04:04,286 --> 00:04:07,792
an experimentally determined CDK20 structure in
their starting workflow.

70
00:04:07,792 --> 00:04:09,740
That is a practical contribution.

71
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:12,468
It does not show exactly how much

72
00:04:12,468 --> 00:04:15,195
time this route saved over every alternative.

73
00:04:15,195 --> 00:04:18,312
More importantly, knowing the target shape still
leaves

74
00:04:18,312 --> 00:04:21,818
a huge question: what new molecule should we
try?

75
00:04:21,818 --> 00:04:24,935
This is where molecular generation enters the
story.

76
00:04:24,935 --> 00:04:28,052
Instead of only sorting a fixed collection of

77
00:04:28,052 --> 00:04:31,558
existing molecules, a generative model can
propose candidate structures.

78
00:04:31,558 --> 00:04:34,675
It learns patterns from chemical data and uses

79
00:04:34,675 --> 00:04:37,792
design information and evaluation criteria to
explore possibilities.

80
00:04:37,792 --> 00:04:40,520
In this study, the team used a

81
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:43,247
platform called Chemistry42 to support that
work.

82
00:04:43,247 --> 00:04:45,974
This is not just a shape-matching puzzle.

83
00:04:45,974 --> 00:04:48,701
Chemical interactions matter too, and
researchers need

84
00:04:48,701 --> 00:04:51,429
structures they can actually make and
investigate.

85
00:04:51,429 --> 00:04:56,493
A design can therefore be promising for one
reason and unsuitable for another.

86
00:04:56,493 --> 00:05:00,389
That is why proposing many structures and
evaluating them are

87
00:05:00,389 --> 00:05:04,675
connected tasks, rather than a machine
announcing a single perfect answer.

88
00:05:04,675 --> 00:05:08,571
It helps to think of this as proposing chemical
hypotheses.

89
00:05:08,571 --> 00:05:12,467
A hypothesis says: this structure might have
properties worth testing.

90
00:05:12,467 --> 00:05:16,364
It does not say: this is the one correct drug.

91
00:05:16,364 --> 00:05:20,260
There may be many different structures that
interact with a

92
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:23,766
target, and each can have different strengths
and weaknesses.

93
00:05:23,766 --> 00:05:27,273
Generating a design also does not manufacture
the substance.

94
00:05:27,273 --> 00:05:30,779
The team then used computational evaluation,
including molecular docking.

95
00:05:30,779 --> 00:05:34,675
Docking explores possible ways a molecule could
sit in the

96
00:05:34,675 --> 00:05:38,182
target pocket and helps assess which designs to
prioritise.

97
00:05:38,182 --> 00:05:42,857
The predicted pose and score are clues, not
proof of real binding.

98
00:05:42,857 --> 00:05:47,532
A convincing-looking fit on a computer still has
to survive an experiment.

99
00:05:47,532 --> 00:05:51,039
In this first design stage, algorithms
contribute before the

100
00:05:51,039 --> 00:05:54,545
laboratory tests, but they do not replace the
laboratory.

101
00:05:54,545 --> 00:05:57,662
AI helps supply structural predictions and new
suggestions.

102
00:05:57,662 --> 00:05:59,610
Other calculations help evaluate them.

103
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:04,676
Researchers bring chemical and biological
judgement to the choice of what to make.

104
00:06:04,676 --> 00:06:08,572
The interesting question is whether this
cooperation can turn a

105
00:06:08,572 --> 00:06:12,468
large set of possibilities into a useful
experimental starting point.

106
00:06:12,468 --> 00:06:16,364
In the first round, the team generated 8,918
structures.

107
00:06:16,364 --> 00:06:19,091
They did not synthesise all of them.

108
00:06:19,091 --> 00:06:22,208
Through computational evaluation, docking and
clustering, they prioritised

109
00:06:22,208 --> 00:06:25,325
54, then selected seven for synthesis and
testing.

110
00:06:25,325 --> 00:06:30,390
Clustering helps organise related designs,
rather than treating every suggestion as
completely separate.

111
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:34,286
Those numbers describe different stages, not
different measures of success.

112
00:06:34,286 --> 00:06:36,624
The 8,918 are generated structures.

113
00:06:36,624 --> 00:06:38,572
The 54 are prioritised candidates.

114
00:06:38,572 --> 00:06:40,909
The seven are selected experimental objects.

115
00:06:40,909 --> 00:06:43,637
Seven selected molecules does not mean seven

116
00:06:43,637 --> 00:06:46,364
successful drugs, or even seven confirmed hits.

117
00:06:46,364 --> 00:06:49,481
Selection tells us what the researchers chose to

118
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:52,208
investigate, not what the experiments would
find.

119
00:06:52,208 --> 00:06:55,714
This is where the potential efficiency benefit
becomes concrete.

120
00:06:55,714 --> 00:06:58,052
Making and testing molecules takes resources.

121
00:06:58,052 --> 00:07:01,169
Better suggestions could help researchers spend
more of

122
00:07:01,169 --> 00:07:04,286
those resources on useful experiments and
follow-up work.

123
00:07:04,286 --> 00:07:07,792
But we should not divide 8,918 by seven

124
00:07:07,792 --> 00:07:10,520
and call the result a speed increase.

125
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:15,195
Nobody established that a comparable alternative
had to make every generated structure.

126
00:07:15,195 --> 00:07:17,922
Traditional discovery is not blind guessing
either.

127
00:07:17,922 --> 00:07:21,429
It also uses existing knowledge, computation and
expert decisions.

128
00:07:21,429 --> 00:07:24,546
The fair question is whether the AI-assisted
route

129
00:07:24,546 --> 00:07:27,273
produces more useful results under comparable
conditions.

130
00:07:27,273 --> 00:07:31,169
Before making that comparison, however, the team
first needed to

131
00:07:31,169 --> 00:07:35,455
show something more basic: did any selected
molecule actually bind CDK20?

132
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:39,351
The experiments did identify a binding hit: a
molecule that

133
00:07:39,351 --> 00:07:42,857
showed binding to CDK20 under the reported test
conditions.

134
00:07:42,857 --> 00:07:47,143
This matters because the story had moved beyond
a computational score.

135
00:07:47,143 --> 00:07:51,429
The team had made a molecule and obtained an
experimental result.

136
00:07:51,429 --> 00:07:54,156
A prediction had led to something that

137
00:07:54,156 --> 00:07:56,883
could be investigated in the real world.

138
00:07:56,883 --> 00:08:00,000
The paper reports 30 days from target selection

139
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,727
to the first experimentally confirmed binding
hit.

140
00:08:02,727 --> 00:08:05,844
That is the milestone behind the striking
number.

141
00:08:05,844 --> 00:08:09,740
It is not 30 days to an approved medicine, and

142
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:13,247
it does not include all the optimisation that
followed.

143
00:08:13,247 --> 00:08:17,143
The reported period also does not mean the team
built

144
00:08:17,143 --> 00:08:21,429
its data resources and AI platforms from scratch
within that time.

145
00:08:21,429 --> 00:08:24,546
We can therefore say the workflow produced an

146
00:08:24,546 --> 00:08:27,273
early result on a short reported timeline.

147
00:08:27,273 --> 00:08:32,338
We cannot yet say how many times faster it was
than another route.

148
00:08:32,338 --> 00:08:37,403
That needs a comparison with the same task and a
similar starting point.

149
00:08:37,403 --> 00:08:42,078
And even this real binding result raised a new
problem: binding to

150
00:08:42,078 --> 00:08:47,143
a protein is not the same as strongly changing
what the protein does.

151
00:08:47,143 --> 00:08:51,818
The first hit had relatively weak functional
inhibition in the enzyme test.

152
00:08:51,818 --> 00:08:54,546
That does not erase the binding result.

153
00:08:54,546 --> 00:08:57,662
It shows that the team had answered one

154
00:08:57,662 --> 00:09:01,169
question but not yet achieved the performance it
wanted.

155
00:09:01,169 --> 00:09:05,844
This distinction is important: a binding test
asks whether the molecule interacts

156
00:09:05,844 --> 00:09:10,909
with the target; a functional test asks whether
it changes the target activity.

157
00:09:10,909 --> 00:09:13,636
The researchers used experimental information
and predicted

158
00:09:13,636 --> 00:09:16,363
binding poses to guide another design round.

159
00:09:16,363 --> 00:09:19,870
They proposed 16 structures and synthesised six
for testing.

160
00:09:19,870 --> 00:09:22,987
The later compounds included molecules with
improved binding.

161
00:09:22,987 --> 00:09:26,493
One compound also underwent further enzyme and
cell evaluation.

162
00:09:26,493 --> 00:09:31,558
These later results belong to follow-up work,
not to the first 30-day milestone.

163
00:09:31,558 --> 00:09:36,233
The follow-up compound, referred to here as 048,
inhibited CDK20 enzyme activity

164
00:09:36,233 --> 00:09:40,519
and reduced Huh7 liver cancer cell viability in
a laboratory assay.

165
00:09:40,519 --> 00:09:43,247
That is additional evidence, but a cell

166
00:09:43,247 --> 00:09:45,974
result still is not a patient result.

167
00:09:45,974 --> 00:09:49,870
It also does not, by itself, prove that every
effect

168
00:09:49,870 --> 00:09:53,377
in the cells came only from acting on CDK20.

169
00:09:53,377 --> 00:09:56,883
Different experiments answer different
questions, and their answers should

170
00:09:56,883 --> 00:10:00,390
not be merged into one claim of treatment
success.

171
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:02,728
Selectivity is another useful word here.

172
00:10:02,728 --> 00:10:05,844
It asks whether a molecule acts on the

173
00:10:05,844 --> 00:10:08,961
intended target rather than also affecting other
targets.

174
00:10:08,961 --> 00:10:12,857
Stronger binding to one protein does not answer
that question.

175
00:10:12,857 --> 00:10:15,974
Likewise, a cell experiment examines a model
under

176
00:10:15,974 --> 00:10:18,701
particular conditions, not the whole human body.

177
00:10:18,701 --> 00:10:21,818
These are reasons to continue studying a hit,

178
00:10:21,818 --> 00:10:24,935
not reasons to ignore the progress already made.

179
00:10:24,935 --> 00:10:28,831
The useful lesson is the loop: propose a design,
test

180
00:10:28,831 --> 00:10:32,338
it, identify what is missing, and revise the
design.

181
00:10:32,338 --> 00:10:35,844
The first imperfection becomes information for
the next decision.

182
00:10:35,844 --> 00:10:38,961
Researchers still need to study selectivity,
what happens

183
00:10:38,961 --> 00:10:42,467
to the molecule in the body and possible
toxicity.

184
00:10:42,467 --> 00:10:45,195
The 2023 candidate study did not report

185
00:10:45,195 --> 00:10:47,922
animal or patient trials for these molecules.

186
00:10:47,922 --> 00:10:51,428
Those experiments give us a basis for discussing
benefits.

187
00:10:51,428 --> 00:10:54,935
Predicted protein structure supported design,
AI-assisted generation proposed molecules,

188
00:10:54,935 --> 00:10:58,441
and experiments identified a hit and supported
further improvement.

189
00:10:58,441 --> 00:11:03,506
This is a contribution to early discovery, not
proof of a finished treatment.

190
00:11:03,506 --> 00:11:06,623
We should separate what this workflow achieved
from

191
00:11:06,623 --> 00:11:09,740
the wider advantages we hope it could bring.

192
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:13,247
This connects to the efficiency idea we care
about.

193
00:11:13,247 --> 00:11:17,143
If algorithms supply more useful designs
earlier, researchers may avoid

194
00:11:17,143 --> 00:11:21,428
some low-value attempts and spend more effort on
validation and optimisation.

195
00:11:21,428 --> 00:11:23,766
That is a meaningful possible benefit.

196
00:11:23,766 --> 00:11:27,662
But this single study does not measure how much
time

197
00:11:27,662 --> 00:11:31,558
or money was saved compared with an equally
resourced alternative.

198
00:11:31,558 --> 00:11:35,844
The achievement and the size of the advantage
are separate questions.

199
00:11:35,844 --> 00:11:40,519
Saving effort early would matter even if later
drug development remained difficult.

200
00:11:40,519 --> 00:11:42,857
A team has limited laboratory capacity.

201
00:11:42,857 --> 00:11:45,974
If useful designs arrive sooner, it may have

202
00:11:45,974 --> 00:11:49,480
more room to test alternatives and learn from
failures.

203
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:53,766
The value is not simply having fewer molecules
on a list.

204
00:11:53,766 --> 00:11:57,273
It is reaching better decisions with available
resources, while

205
00:11:57,273 --> 00:12:00,779
retaining enough variety to avoid overlooking
other promising directions.

206
00:12:00,779 --> 00:12:04,285
The wider hopes include health, cost and animal
welfare.

207
00:12:04,285 --> 00:12:07,013
Earlier useful candidates might support future
treatments,

208
00:12:07,013 --> 00:12:09,740
and avoiding low-value experiments might save
resources.

209
00:12:09,740 --> 00:12:14,415
Better predictions might also help researchers
consider alternatives to some animal
experiments.

210
00:12:14,415 --> 00:12:17,922
These are possibilities, not measured outcomes
of this case.

211
00:12:17,922 --> 00:12:21,039
We would need evidence of actual savings and

212
00:12:21,039 --> 00:12:24,545
suitable validation, not simply a shorter list
of molecules.

213
00:12:24,545 --> 00:12:26,883
Environmental claims need the same care.

214
00:12:26,883 --> 00:12:30,000
Fewer unnecessary syntheses could mean less
material use

215
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,727
and waste, but computers also consume
electricity.

216
00:12:32,727 --> 00:12:36,623
We should compare the resources used by both the
calculations

217
00:12:36,623 --> 00:12:40,130
and the laboratory work for a similar research
outcome.

218
00:12:40,130 --> 00:12:44,026
Without that comparison, this case does not
establish that AI

219
00:12:44,026 --> 00:12:47,922
is cheaper, reduces animal use or is
environmentally better overall.

220
00:12:47,922 --> 00:12:51,818
A stronger next study could compare methods on
the same

221
00:12:51,818 --> 00:12:55,325
target, with similar information, resources and
definitions of success.

222
00:12:55,325 --> 00:12:58,052
It should record time to a confirmed

223
00:12:58,052 --> 00:13:00,779
hit and experimental effort, including
unsuccessful attempts.

224
00:13:00,779 --> 00:13:03,506
A second improvement is to use new

225
00:13:03,506 --> 00:13:06,233
experimental feedback to check and revise
predictions.

226
00:13:06,233 --> 00:13:10,130
A third is to evaluate several properties
together, so better

227
00:13:10,130 --> 00:13:13,636
binding does not hide poor selectivity or other
problems.

228
00:13:13,636 --> 00:13:15,974
Our sources have different jobs too.

229
00:13:15,974 --> 00:13:20,649
The original study and its supplement support
the case numbers and experiments.

230
00:13:20,649 --> 00:13:23,376
Method papers explain what the tools do.

231
00:13:23,376 --> 00:13:27,662
Scholarly books and reviews help explain the
concepts and wider context.

232
00:13:27,662 --> 00:13:31,168
A platform developer describing its own work is
useful

233
00:13:31,168 --> 00:13:34,675
evidence, but not the same as an independent
comparison.

234
00:13:34,675 --> 00:13:38,571
We need to evaluate the source, not just count
references.

235
00:13:38,571 --> 00:13:40,909
This matters outside a research laboratory.

236
00:13:40,909 --> 00:13:44,026
When we hear that AI has discovered a

237
00:13:44,026 --> 00:13:47,532
drug, we can ask what the headline actually
means.

238
00:13:47,532 --> 00:13:50,649
Was it a predicted structure, a laboratory hit,

239
00:13:50,649 --> 00:13:53,766
a result in cells or evidence from patients?

240
00:13:53,766 --> 00:13:57,662
Understanding those stages helps us judge the
news without dismissing

241
00:13:57,662 --> 00:14:01,948
genuine progress or expecting a treatment that
has not been demonstrated.

242
00:14:01,948 --> 00:14:04,675
We can now answer our opening question.

243
00:14:04,675 --> 00:14:07,403
Can we predict whether a molecule is

244
00:14:07,403 --> 00:14:10,130
worth testing before we even make it?

245
00:14:10,130 --> 00:14:14,026
We can make informed predictions, but
experiments must test them.

246
00:14:14,026 --> 00:14:17,143
In this case, algorithms helped people propose
and

247
00:14:17,143 --> 00:14:20,260
prioritise designs, while experiments guided the
next step.

248
00:14:20,260 --> 00:14:22,597
The promise is a better-directed search.

249
00:14:22,597 --> 00:14:26,494
How much faster that search becomes, and whether
it leads

250
00:14:26,494 --> 00:14:30,000
to a useful medicine, remain questions for
further evidence.
